{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SzRwMvZ6irGmub2npPSUCV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/bin/bash: line 1: cd: kit: No such file or directory\n2\t### class Engine\n3\t\n4\t- `static readonly version: string`: The engine's version (package.json).\n5\t- `static start(game: Game, options: EngineOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`\n6\t- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listeners, no AudioContext (`audio.counts` still counts), no panels; errors go to `engin…\n7\t- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the first one first. (`step()` throw…\n8\t- `readonly input: Input`\n9\t- `readonly sun: DirectionalLight`\n10\t- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\n11\t- `readonly context: GameContext`\n12\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\n13\t- `readonly particles: Particles`\n14\t- `readonly hud: Hud`\n15\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\n16\t- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\n17\t- `debug: DebugUI | null`\n18\t- `touch: TouchControls | null`\n19\t- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\n20\t- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\n21\t- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\n22\t- `frame: number`\n23\t- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\n24\t- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\n25\t- `time: number`\n26\t- `readonly renderer: View`\n27\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n28\t- `camera: CameraRig`\n29\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n30\t- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\n31\t- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\n32\t- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\n33\t- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\n34\t- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\n35\t- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\n36\t- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\n37\t- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\n38\t- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\n39\t- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\n40\t- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\n41\t- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\n42\t- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n43\t- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\n44\t- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\n45\t- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\n46\t- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\n47\t- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\n48\t- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\n49\t- `get filters: readonly string[]`\n50\t- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\n51\t- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\n52\t- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\n53\t- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\n54\t- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\n55\t- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\n56\t- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\n57\t- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\n58\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\n59\t- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\n60\t- `get random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`).\n61\t- `recording(): Recording | null`: The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\n62\t- `replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o: { frames?: number } = {}): Promise<void>`: Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manua…\n63\t- `snapshot(): Promise<Snapshot>`: The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the game's own state (`Game.snapshot`). Plain JSON. Taken once the las…\n64\t- `restore(saved: Snapshot): Promise<void>`: Put the running level back to a snapshot of it: the same game (by name), already loaded; nothing in between is replayed. The level's recording starts again from it (`Recording.from`), so what is played next replays with `replay()`. Live input held now is let…\n65\t- `fingerprint(): string`: A hash of the simulation: game time, every physics body (exact), `ctx.random`'s state and the game's own (`Game.fingerprint`). Two runs that should be the same (a replay) compare equal. Not the `status()` line: a debug line may carry measurements (a step's mi…\n66\t\n67\t### interface Game\n68\t\n69\tA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\n70\tlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\n71\t\n72\t- `readonly name: string`\n73\t- `readonly args?: unknown`: The plain JSON the game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by): kept in its recordings (`Recording.args`), so a tool can make the same game again to replay one (`npm run replay`, src/games.…\n74\t- `readonly assets?: readonly string[]`: Models `setup` will load (`ctx.loadModel` URLs). Optional: listing them lets the engine start downloading while the renderer and physics initialise.\n75\t- `setup(ctx: GameContext): Promise<void> | void`: Build the level: load models, add colliders, spawn entities.\n76\t- `fixedUpdate?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs before every fixed 60 Hz physics step. Move characters/apply forces here.\n77\t- `ready?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is…\n78\t- `update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state).\n79\t- `cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: World position the camera follows.\n80\t- `eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up).\n81\t- `status?(ctx: GameContext): string`: One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...).\n82\t- `fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string`: The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay mu…\n83\t- `snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown`: The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\n84\t- `restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>`: Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\n85\t- `beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>`: Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `re…\n86\t- `onCameraChange?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world, physics and player are untouched; adjust anything that depends on the preset (e.g. side-scroller lane lock, hiding the player model in first person).\n87\t- `dispose?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called when the game is unloaded (`engine.loadGame(other)` or `engine.dispose()`), before the engine removes its scene objects, physics bodies, triggers, particles, HUD and music. Free anything else the game owns: DOM, timers, listeners, HMR hooks.\n88\t\n89\t### interface GameContext\n90\t\n91\t- `readonly engine: Engine`\n92\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n93\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n94\t- `readonly input: Input`\n95\t- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n96\t- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n97\t- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n98\t- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n99\t- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n100\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n101\t- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n102\t- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n103\t- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n104\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n105\t\n106\t### class Input\n107\t\n108\tKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n109\t- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n110\t  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n111\t- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n112\t  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n113\t  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n114\t- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n115\t  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n116\t  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n117\t  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n118\t  a frame is not lost.\n119\t- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n120\t\n121\t- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n122\t- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n123\t- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n124\t- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n125\t- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n126\t- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n127\t- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n128\t- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n129\t- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n130\t- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n131\t- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n132\t- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n133\t- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n134\t- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n135\t- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n136\t- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n137\t- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n138\t- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n139\t- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n140\t- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n141\t- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n142\t- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n143\t- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n144\t- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n145\t- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n146\t- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n147\t- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n148\t- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n149\t- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n150\t- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n151\t- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n152\t- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n153\t- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n154\t- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n155\t- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n156\t- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n157\t- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play read…\n158\t- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keep…\n159\t- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n160\t- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n161\t- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n162\t- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n163\t- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n164\t- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n165\t\n166\t### interface InputFrame\n167\t\n168\tWhat the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\n169\tOptional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\n170\tfrom the frame before.\n171\t\n172\t- `dt: number`: Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it).\n173\t- `held?: string[]`: Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted.\n174\t- `pressed?: string[]`: Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held).\n175\t- `analog?: [number, number]`: The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone).\n176\t- `pad?: [number, number]`\n177\t- `delta?: [number, number]`: Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame.\n178\t- `wheel?: number`\n179\t- `pointer?: [number, number, number, number]`: Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says).\n180\t### class AudioManager\n181\t\n182\tWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\n183\tsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\n184\tpersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n185\t\n186\tBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\n187\tfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n188\t(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\n189\twas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n190\t\n191\t- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n192\t- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n193\t- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n194\t- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n195\t- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n196\t- `context: AudioContext | null`\n197\t- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n198\t- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n199\t- `get muted: boolean`\n200\t- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n201\t- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n202\t- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n203\t- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n204\t- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n205\t- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n206\t- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n207\t- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n208\t- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n209\t- `stopMusic(): void`\n210\t- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n211\t- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n212\t- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n213\t- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n214\t- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n215\t- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n216\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n217\t\n218\t### interface AudioSettings\n219\t\n220\t- `master: number`\n221\t- `sfx: number`\n222\t- `music: number`\n223\t- `muted: boolean`\n224\t\n225\t### type Instrument\n226\t\n227\t```ts\n228\ttype Instrument = Omit<SoundDef, 'freq' | 'freqEnd' | 'sustain' | 'delay'>\n229\t```\n230\t\n231\tA track's instrument: a SoundDef without pitch or length (each note sets those).\n232\t\n233\t### interface Listener\n234\t\n235\t- `position: V3Like`\n236\t- `right: V3Like`: The listener's right, in world space (unit): the camera's right for a third-person game.\n237\t\n238\t### muffleCutoff\n239\t\n240\t```ts\n241\tconst muffleCutoff: (muffle: number) => number\n242\t```\n243\t\n244\tLow-pass cutoff (Hz) for a muffle of 0..1: wide open to a dull thud.\n245\t\n246\t### noteFrequency()\n247\t\n248\t```ts\n249\tfunction noteFrequency(note: string): number | null\n250\t```\n251\t\n252\t'A4' → 440, 'C#5', 'Eb3'… Returns null for anything that is not a note.\n253\t\n254\t### parseSong()\n255\t\n256\t```ts\n257\tfunction parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong\n258\t```\n259\t\n260\tFlatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos.\n261\t\n262\t### PLAYGROUND_SONG\n263\t\n264\t```ts\n265\tconst PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song\n266\t```\n267\t\n268\tThe playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps.\n269\t\n270\t### interface PlayOptions\n271\t\n272\t- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n273\t- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo.\n274\t- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n275\t\n276\t### renderSound()\n277\t\n278\t```ts\n279\tfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n280\t```\n281\t\n282\tRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n283\t\n284\t### SFX\n285\t\n286\t```ts\n287\tconst SFX: { jump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; attack: number; sustain: number; decay: number; volume: number; }; doubleJump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; ... 5 more ...; volume: number; }; ... 8 more ...; fanfare: { ...; }; }\n288\t```\n289\t\n290\tBuilt-in retro sound effects, as data. Tweak numbers here (or register your own with\n291\t`ctx.audio.register(name, def)`) and every `ctx.audio.play(name)` picks them up.\n292\tSee docs/engine/audio.md for the SoundDef fields.\n293\t\n294\t### type SfxName\n295\t\n296\t```ts\n297\ttype SfxName = keyof typeof SFX\n298\t```\n299\t\n300\t### interface Song\n301\t\n302\tA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\n303\twhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\n304\tEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\n305\tnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n306\t\n307\t- `bpm: number`\n308\t- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n309\t- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n310\t- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n311\t- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n312\t- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n313\t\n314\t### interface SoundDef\n315\t\n316\t- `wave: Wave`\n317\t- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n318\t- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n319\t- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n320\t- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n321\t- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n322\t- `sustain?: number`\n323\t- `decay?: number`\n324\t- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n325\t- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n326\t- `arpRate?: number`\n327\t- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n328\t- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n329\t- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n330\t- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n331\t\n332\t### interface Spatial\n333\t\n334\t- `gain: number`: 0..1 volume.\n335\t- `pan: number`: -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n336\t- `distance: number`\n337\t\n338\t### spatialize()\n339\t### class Hud\n340\t\n341\tPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\n342\tthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\n343\tlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n344\t(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n345\t\n346\tRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n347\t(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\n348\tresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n349\t\n350\t  ctx.hud.clear();\n351\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n352\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n353\t\n354\t- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n355\t- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n356\t- `visible: boolean`\n357\t- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n358\t- `get height: number`\n359\t- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n360\t- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n361\t- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n362\t- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n363\t- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n364\t- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n365\t- `dispose(): void`\n366\t\n367\t### type HudAnchor\n368\t\n369\t```ts\n370\ttype HudAnchor = 'top-left' | 'top' | 'top-right' | 'left' | 'center' | 'right' | 'bottom-left' | 'bottom' | 'bottom-right'\n371\t```\n372\t\n373\tWhere an element is attached on screen; it is also aligned that way (right-anchored text grows left).\n374\t\n375\t### type HudColor\n376\t\n377\t```ts\n378\ttype HudColor = PaletteColor | number\n379\t```\n380\t\n381\t### interface HudOptions\n382\t\n383\t- `color?: HudColor`: Default 'white'.\n384\t- `shadow?: HudColor | false`: 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'.\n385\t- `scale?: number`: Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1.\n386\t- `anchor?: HudAnchor`: Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels.\n387\t\n388\t### hudPlace()\n389\t\n390\t```ts\n391\tfunction hudPlace(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, anchor: HudAnchor, size: Resolution): [number, number]\n392\t```\n393\t\n394\tTop-left corner, in whole art pixels, of a w×h element placed at offset (x, y) from\n395\t`anchor` on a screen of `size` art pixels. Pure (unit-tested).\n396\t\n397\t## 11. Models and textures (ASSETS.md)\n398\t\n399\t### addModelColliders()\n400\t\n401\t```ts\n402\tfunction addModelColliders(physics: Physics, build: Pick<ModelBuild, 'colliders'>, at: Vec3 = [0, 0, 0], yaw = 0): RAPIER.Collider[]\n403\t```\n404\t\n405\tStatic Rapier colliders for a placed model (its `colliders` data), at `at` turned `yaw`\n406\tradians about Y: a box, an upright cylinder or a convex hull, with `addStaticBox`'s friction.\n407\t\n408\t### around()\n409\t\n410\t```ts\n411\tfunction around(n: number, part: Each<[i: number]>, o: { r?: number; phase?: number } = {}): Part[]\n412\t```\n413\t\n414\t`n` copies turned about Y, `360 / n` degrees apart from `phase`. `r` first pushes each copy\n415\tout along its own +Z; with the default 0 the part's own `at` places it.\n416\t\n417\t### assetProgress()\n418\t### interface ClipDef\n419\t\n420\t- `name: string`\n421\t- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n422\t- `loop?: boolean`\n423\t- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n424\t- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n425\t- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instead of sinking.\n426\t- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n427\t- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n428\t- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n429\t- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n430\t- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n431\t- `notes?: string`\n432\t\n433\t### interface ClipReport\n434\t\n435\tObjective checks for a clip, computed by actually posing the rig (no renderer). These\n436\tare the numbers an agent can't eyeball: foot sliding, ground contact, loop seams,\n437\tvelocity spikes, joint limits. Thresholds are deliberately forgiving; `problems` are\n438\treal defects, `warnings` are worth a look.\n439\t\n440\t- `name: string`\n441\t- `frames: number`\n442\t- `loop: boolean`\n443\t- `speed: number | null`\n444\t- `minSoleY: number`: Lowest sole point over the clip (m) and the frame it happens. Negative = through the floor.\n445\t- `minSoleFrame: number`\n446\t- `minBodyY: number`: Lowest point of any other body part (knees, hands, head...), where and when.\n447\t- `minBodyMesh: string`\n448\t- `minBodyFrame: number`\n449\t- `floatingFrames: number`: Frames (of grounded clips) where neither sole is within 5 cm of the floor.\n450\t- `footSlide: number`: Mean horizontal slip of the planted foot, m/s (0 = perfectly planted).\n451\t- `loopSeam: number`: Largest joint-angle jump between the last and first frame of a loop, degrees.\n452\t- `maxAngularSpeed: { joint: string; degPerSec: number; frame: number }`: Fastest joint rotation, degrees per second, and where.\n453\t- `limitViolations: { joint: string; axis: 'x' | 'y' | 'z'; frame: number; value: number; range: [number, number] }[]`\n454\t- `pelvisY: [number, number]`: Pelvis height range (m) — how much the body bobs.\n455\t- `problems: string[]`\n456\t- `warnings: string[]`\n457\t\n458\t### compileClip()\n459\t\n460\t```ts\n461\tfunction compileClip(def: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec, rest: RestPose): AnimationClip\n462\t```\n463\t\n464\tBake a ClipDef into a three.js AnimationClip (quaternion + position + scale tracks,\n465\tsampled at 60 Hz so easing and per-axis Euler interpolation — including 360° flips —\n466\tsurvive exactly).\n467\t\n468\t### compileClips()\n469\t\n470\t```ts\n471\tfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n472\t```\n473\t\n474\t### interface Foot\n475\t\n476\t- `readonly at: Vec3`: World position of the foot now.\n477\t- `t: number`: Stepping: 0 → 1 through the step; -1 planted.\n478\t- `readonly from: Vec3`\n479\t- `readonly to: Vec3`\n480\t\n481\t### interface FootGoal\n482\t\n483\tFoot-placement IK: say where a foot should be, get the hip/knee/ankle rotations.\n484\t\n485\tAuthors write the body (pelvis height, lean, arms...) and then pin the feet:\n486\t\n487\t  placeFeet({ Pelvis: { p: [0, -0.2, 0] }, Torso: [20, 0, 0] }, rig, {\n488\t    R: { z: 0.15 },                             // right foot flat, 15 cm ahead\n489\t    L: { z: -0.2, pitch: 30, pivot: 'ball' },   // left heel raised on its toes\n490\t  })\n491\t\n492\tCoordinates are the character's ground frame: y up from the floor, z forward. The solve\n493\tis planar (legs bend about X, knees forward); lateral offsets are ignored. It accounts\n494\tfor the root's position, rotation and squash, so a squashed or leaning body still\n495\tkeeps its feet exactly where they were asked…\n496\t\n497\t- `z: number`: Forward position of the ankle (m) when the foot is flat.\n498\t- `x?: number`: Sideways position (m, + = the character's left). Default: under the hip at rest. Only matters when the root is turned (e.g. a pivot on one foot); the solve itself stays in the leg's plane.\n499\t- `y?: number`: Height of the sole above the floor (m). 0 = standing on it.\n500\t- `pitch?: number`: Sole pitch in degrees: + = toes down / heel raised, − = toes up.\n501\t- `pivot?: 'heel' | 'ball' | 'ankle'`: Point that stays at (z, y) while pitching: heel (heel strike), toe tip (tiptoe, toe-off) or ankle.\n502\t- `follow?: number`: 0..1: let the foot hang from the shin instead of holding its world pitch (swinging feet, dangling in the air). 1 = foot at `relax` degrees relative to the shin.\n503\t- `relax?: number`: Foot angle relative to the shin when following (default 15 = toes slightly down).\n504\t\n505\t### gaitClip()\n506\t### validateClip()\n507\t\n508\t```ts\n509\tfunction validateClip(clip: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec): string[]\n510\t```\n511\t\n512\tProblems with a clip definition itself (unknown joints, bad key order, loop mismatch).\n513\t\n514\t## 13. Hero kit\n515\t\n516\t### HERO_CLIPS\n517\t\n518\t```ts\n519\t### applySky()\n520\t\n521\t```ts\n522\tfunction applySky(engine: Pick<Engine, 'sun' | 'ambient' | 'scene' | 'setSunDirection'>, s: SkyState): void\n523\t```\n524\t\n525\tPut a sky on the engine: sun direction, colour and strength, ambient light, background.\n526\t\n527\t### bayer4()\n528\t\n529\t### class Decals\n530\t\n531\t- `new Decals(scene: Scene, o: { capacity?: number } = {})`\n532\t- `readonly meshes: Map<\"ring\" | \"crack\" | \"footprint\" | \"scorch\" | \"splat\", InstancedMesh<BufferGeometry<NormalBufferAttributes, BufferGeometryEventMap>, Material<...> | Material…`\n533\t- `readonly o: { capacity?: number }`\n534\t- `add(shape: DecalShape, at: Vector3Like, normal: Vector3Like, o: DecalOptions = {}): number`: Lay a mark at `at` on a surface facing `normal`. Returns its index in that shape's pool.\n535\t- `count(shape?: DecalShape): number`: Marks alive (per shape or all).\n536\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Per frame: age the marks; old ones dither away over a second.\n537\t- `clear(): void`: Remove every mark (the meshes stay for reuse).\n538\t- `dispose(): void`\n539\t\n540\t### type DecalShape\n541\t\n542\t```ts\n543\ttype DecalShape = (typeof DECAL_SHAPES)[number]\n544\t```\n545\t\n546\t### decodeState()\n547\t\n548\t```ts\n549\tfunction decodeState(v: unknown): unknown\n550\t```\n551\t\n552\t`encodeState` undone: a deep copy with the marked numbers (and the game's own `@` strings) back.\n553\t\n554\t### drawSheet()\n555\t### class Precipitation\n556\t\n557\t- `new Precipitation(o: PrecipitationOptions)`\n558\t- `readonly kind: PrecipitationKind`\n559\t- `readonly area: readonly [number, number, number]`\n560\t- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind (m/s along x and z): rain slants, snow drifts.\n561\t- `get intensity: number`: Share of the drops falling (0..1).\n562\t- `get speed: number`: Fall speed (m/s).\n563\t- `update(dt: number, center: Vector3): void`: Per frame (game time): advance the fall and follow `center`.\n564\t- `splashes(dt: number): number`: How many drops hit the ground in `dt` seconds at this intensity (for splash particles, ripples).\n565\t- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n566\t\n567\t### type PrecipitationKind\n568\t\n569\t```ts\n570\ttype PrecipitationKind = 'rain' | 'snow'\n571\t```\n572\t\n573\t### interface PrecipitationOptions\n574\t\n575\t- `kind: PrecipitationKind`\n576\t- `count: number`: Most drops at once (the instance count).\n577\t- `area?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Width, height and depth of the air the drops fall through, around the centre.\n578\t- `color?: number`: Drop colour (hex).\n579\t- `speed?: number`: Fall speed (m/s). Rain 16, snow 1.4.\n580\t\n581\t### interface Recording\n582\t\n583\t- `readonly format: 1`\n584\t- `readonly engine: string`: The engine version that recorded it (a different one may not replay the same).\n585\t- `readonly game: string`: The game's name when it was recorded.\n586\t- `readonly args?: unknown`: What the game was made with (`Game.args`), when its name alone can't rebuild it.\n587\t- `readonly seed: number`: The engine seed (`ctx.random` comes from it and the game's name).\n588\t- `readonly time: number`: Game time when the level began (`ctx.time` carries on across levels).\n589\t- `readonly frames: readonly InputFrame[]`: Every frame the level ran, from its first.\n590\t- `readonly truncated?: boolean`: True when the level ran longer than a recording keeps (the frames are its start).\n591\t- `readonly from?: Snapshot`: The save-state the frames start from (`engine.restore()` began this recording), if any.\n592\t- `readonly view?: Viewport`: The viewport it was recorded on, as it was when the level began (CSS pixels and DPR): the art resolution, pointer aim and the HUD's layout follow it. A headless replay frames for it; a page warns when its own differs. A page resized during the level is not fo…\n593\t\n594\t### recordingProblem()\n595\t\n596\t```ts\n597\tfunction recordingProblem(v: unknown): string | null\n598\t```\n599\t\n600\tIs `v` a recording this engine can replay? Returns the problem, or null.\n601\t\n602\t### skyAt()\n603\t\n604\t```ts\n605\tfunction skyAt(hour: number): SkyState\n606\t```\n607\t\n608\tThe sky at `hour` (wraps around 24).\n609\t\n610\t### interface SkyState\n611\t\n612\t- `readonly sunDir: [number, number, number]`: Unit vector toward the sun (or the moon, at night).\n613\t- `readonly sunColor: number`\n614\t- `readonly sunIntensity: number`\n615\t- `readonly ambientColor: number`\n616\t- `readonly ambientIntensity: number`\n617\t- `readonly sky: number`: Background (sky) and fog colour.\n618\t- `readonly night: number`: 0 by day, 1 deep at night: lanterns, stars, windows.\n619\t\n620\t### interface Snapshot\n621\t\n622\t- `readonly format: 1`\n623\t- `readonly engine: string`: The engine version that took it (a different one may not restore the same).\n624\t- `readonly game: string`: The game's name: a snapshot restores into the same game.\n625\t- `readonly seed: number`: The engine seed and game time when it was taken.\n626\t- `readonly time: number`\n627\t- `readonly clock: ClockState`\n628\t- `readonly random: number`: `ctx.random`'s state.\n629\t- `readonly physics: PhysicsState`\n630\t- `readonly camera: CameraRigState`\n631\t- `readonly shake: ShakeState`\n632\t- `readonly input: InputState`\n633\t- `readonly state: unknown`: The game's own state (`Game.snapshot`), as JSON.\n634\t\n635\t### snapshotProblem()\n636\t\n637\t```ts\n638\tfunction snapshotProblem(v: unknown): string | null\n639\t```\n640\t\n641\tIs `v` a snapshot this engine can restore? Returns the problem, or null.\n642\t\n643\t### class SoftMesh\n644\t\n645\t- `new SoftMesh(body: VerletBody, material: Material, o: { uv?: Float32Array; doubleSided?: boolean } = {})`\n646\t- `readonly body: VerletBody`\n647\t- `sync(): void`: Copy the particles into the mesh (call once per frame after stepping the body).\n648\t\n649\t### class SpriteBatch\n650\t### swayObject()\n651\t\n652\t```ts\n653\tfunction swayObject(root: Object3D, wind: WindUniforms, o: SwayOptions): void\n654\t```\n655\t\n656\tMake every mesh under `root` sway (its materials replaced by swaying copies).\n657\t\n658\t### interface SwayOptions\n659\t\n660\t- `height: number`: Height (in the mesh's own units) of its top: the lean is 0 at `base` and full there.\n661\t- `base?: number`: Where the lean starts (the trunk below stays still). Default 0.\n662\t- `amount?: number`: Lean at the top per unit of wind. Default 0.15.\n663\t- `phase?: number`: Its own phase (radians): neighbours don't move in lockstep.\n664\t\n665\t### type Team\n666\t### class WaterSurface\n667\t\n668\t- `new WaterSurface(o: WaterOptions)`\n669\t- `readonly size: readonly [number, number]`\n670\t- `waves: Wave[]`\n671\t- `readonly ripples: RippleField | null`\n672\t- `readonly uOpacity: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n673\t- `readonly uFoamAt: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n674\t- `setWaves(waves: readonly Wave[]): void`: Change the waves (up to MAX_WAVES; the CPU height follows too).\n675\t- `update(time: number, sun?: Vector3): void`: Per frame: the waves' clock (use physics time: floaters read the same), the ripples, the sun.\n676\t- `heightAt(x: number, z: number, t = this.time): number`: World height of the drawn surface at (x, z) (waves and ripples), at the last `update` time or `t`.\n677\t- `covers(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is (x, z) over this water?\n678\t- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n679\t\n680\t### class Wfc\n681\t\n682\t- `new Wfc(tiles: readonly WfcTile[], width: number, height: number, o: WfcOptions = {})`\n683\t- `readonly tiles: readonly WfcTile[]`\n684\t- `readonly wave: Uint8Array`: Options still open per cell: `wave[cell * tiles + t]` is 1 while tile t is possible.\n685\t- `readonly options: Uint16Array`: How many options each cell has left.\n686\t- `readonly result: Int32Array`: The chosen tile per cell (−1 until it has one option left).\n687\t- `attempt: number`: Starts so far (1 + the contradictions met).\n688\t- `done: boolean`: True once every cell has one tile, false while solving or after giving up.\n689\t- `failed: boolean`\n690\t- `choices: number`: Cells collapsed by choice (the rest followed from propagation).\n691\t- `readonly width: number`\n692\t- `readonly height: number`\n693\t- `restart(): void`: Start again from scratch (each start after the first uses the next seed).\n694\t- `run(): boolean`: Solve to the end (restarting on contradictions). True if every cell has a tile.\n695\t- `step(): boolean`: One collapse: the cell with the least entropy gets one tile, and that is propagated. Returns false when there is nothing left to do (solved, or given up).\n696\t- `entropy(c: number): number`: Shannon entropy of a cell's options, by weight (0 when it has one left).\n697\t\n698\t### interface WfcOptions\n699\t\n700\t- `seed?: number`\n701\t- `border?: string`: The socket every edge on the grid's outside must be (a closed map: '###'). Default: any.\n702\t- `fixed?: Readonly<Record<number, number>>`: Cells set before solving: cell index (x + y × width) → tile index (they may break the border: a way in).\n703\t- `attempts?: number`: Starts allowed before giving up on contradictions (default 30).\n704\t\n705\t### interface WfcTile\n706\t\n707\t- `readonly name: string`\n708\t- `readonly edges: readonly [string, string, string, string]`: North (−z, up the map), east (+x), south (+z), west (−x).\n709\t- `readonly weight?: number`: How often it is chosen, relative to the others (default 1).\n710\t\n711\t### class WindUniforms\n712\t\n713\t- `readonly time: any`\n714\t- `readonly wind: any`\n715\t- `update(time: number, wind: Vector2 | readonly [number, number]): void`: Per frame (game time); `wind` along x and z (its length is the strength).\n716\t\n717\t### woodMaterial()\n718\t\n719\t```ts\n720\tfunction woodMaterial(o: ShaderOptions = {}): MeshToonNodeMaterial\n721\t### class Rng\n722\t\n723\tSeeded randomness. Everything random in a game comes from an `Rng`, so the same seed always\n724\tgives the same result (docs/DOCTRINE.md, principle 1). Each level gets one, `ctx.random`,\n725\tseeded from the engine's seed and the game's name; fork it by purpose so streams stay\n726\tindependent of how many numbers each one drew:\n727\t\n728\t  const level = ctx.random.fork(`level:${n}`);\n729\t  const room = level.fork(`room:${i}`);   // independent of how many numbers `level` drew\n730\t\n731\tNever use Math.random in game or engine code (the lint refuses it).\n732\t\n733\t- `new Rng(seed: number | string)`\n734\t- `get state: number`: The generator's whole state: save it, and set it back to draw the same numbers again.\n735\t- `next(): number`: Uniform in [0, 1). mulberry32.\n736\t- `fork(purpose: string | number): Rng`: A child generator for one purpose; does not advance this one.\n737\t- `range(min: number, max: number): number`: Float in [min, max).\n738\t- `int(min: number, max: number): number`: Integer in [min, max] (inclusive).\n739\t- `chance(p: number): boolean`\n740\t- `pick(items: readonly T[]): T`\n741\t- `weighted(items: readonly T[], weight: (item: T) => number): T`: Pick by weight; entries with weight <= 0 never come up.\n742\t- `shuffle(items: T[]): T[]`\n743\t- `gaussian(mean = 0, sd = 1): number`: Normal distribution (Box–Muller).\n744\t\n745\t### class RopeMesh\n746\t\n747\t- `new RopeMesh(body: VerletBody, material: Material, thickness = 0.06)`\n748\t- `readonly body: VerletBody`\n749\t- `sync(): void`\n750\t\n751\t### SCREEN_LAYER\n752\t\n753\t```ts\n754\tconst SCREEN_LAYER: 2\n755\t```\n756\t\n757\tThe layer for screens and mirrors: drawn by the main camera, never by a RenderView.\n758\t\n759\t### class ScreenFx\n760\t### class ScreenFx\n761\t\n762\t- `cover(kind: TransitionKind = 'fade', o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover the picture (progress → 1). Resolves when it stops: `true` when it is fully covered, `false` when another transition (or `set`, `clear`) took over first.\n763\t- `reveal(kind?: TransitionKind, o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Uncover it (progress → 0) with `kind` (default: the one that covered it). Resolves like `cover`.\n764\t- `transition(kind: TransitionKind, between: () => unknown, o: TransitionOptions & { revealCenter?: TransitionOptions['center'] } = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover, run `between` (load a level, teleport), then reveal, also when `between` throws (the error is passed on). Resolves to whether the cover completed.\n765\t- `set(kind: TransitionKind | null, progress: number): void`: Jump straight to a progress (0 clear, 1 covered), no animation.\n766\t- `flash(color: ScreenColor = 'white', o: FlashOptions = {}): void`: A full-screen flash that fades out.\n767\t- `shockwave(at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number], o: ShockwaveOptions = {}): void`: A ring that pushes the picture outwards from `at` (oldest ring is replaced when 4 run).\n768\t- `clear(): void`: Stop everything (a clear picture).\n769\t- `state(): ScreenFxState`\n770\t- `update(dt: number, camera: Camera | null): void`: Engine, every rendered frame (real seconds; `camera` projects world centres).\n771\t- `sampleNode(tex: any, scale: any): any`: Sample an art-resolution picture for the device pass, pushed by the shockwaves and blocked up by a mosaic transition, with the rings' bright edge. `tex` is the art target (a texture node); `scale` is device pixels per art pixel.\n772\t- `colorNode(color: any, scale: any): any`: The transition and the flash over a finished colour (device pass).\n773\t\n774\t### interface ScreenFxState\n775\t\n776\tWhat `state()` reports (tests, tools).\n777\t\n778\t- `transition: TransitionKind | null`\n779\t- `progress: number`: 0 clear … 1 covered.\n780\t- `moving: boolean`: A transition is animating.\n781\t- `flash: number`\n782\t- `shockwaves: number`\n783\t\n784\t### interface ShaderOptions\n785\t\n786\t- `color?: number`\n787\t- `time?: any`: The clock it animates by (a float node). Default three's `time`, in seconds.\n788\t\n789\t### interface ShakeState\n790\t\n791\tA shake as data.\n792\t\n793\t- `readonly trauma: number`\n794\t- `readonly t: number`\n795\t- `readonly offset: readonly number[]`\n796\t- `readonly applied: readonly number[]`\n797\t\n798\t### interface ShockwaveOptions\n799\t\n800\t- `radius?: number`: Final radius in screen heights. Default 0.45.\n801\t- `duration?: number`: Seconds (real time). Default 0.6.\n802\t- `strength?: number`: Push strength (1 = a strong blast). Default 1.\n803\t\n804\t### skyAt()\n805\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f147b5a-afdb-41c5-bbfc-62264f8c7cee","uuid":"547362ba-dcc1-4714-94fb-5ae314c706f8","timestamp":"2026-11-06T00:21:19.144Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-171821--x10629c-work/3f147b5a-afdb-41c5-bbfc-62264f8c7cee/tool-results/bhob97uh9.txt","content":"/bin/bash: line 1: cd: kit: No such file or directory\n### class Engine\n\n- `static readonly version: string`: The engine's version (package.json).\n- `static start(game: Game, options: EngineOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`\n- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listeners, no AudioContext (`audio.counts` still counts), no panels; errors go to `engin…\n- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the first one first. (`step()` throw…\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly sun: DirectionalLight`\n- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\n- `readonly context: GameContext`\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\n- `readonly particles: Particles`\n- `readonly hud: Hud`\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\n- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\n- `debug: DebugUI | null`\n- `touch: TouchControls | null`\n- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\n- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\n- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\n- `frame: number`\n- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\n- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\n- `time: number`\n- `readonly renderer: View`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\n- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\n- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\n- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\n- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\n- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\n- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\n- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\n- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\n- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\n- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\n- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\n- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\n- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\n- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\n- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\n- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\n- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\n- `get filters: readonly string[]`\n- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\n- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\n- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\n- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\n- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\n- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\n- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\n- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\n- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\n- `get random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`).\n- `recording(): Recording | null`: The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\n- `replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o: { frames?: number } = {}): Promise<void>`: Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manua…\n- `snapshot(): Promise<Snapshot>`: The running level as data, between frames (docs/engine/determinism.md *Save-states*): game time, the clock, `ctx.random`, the physics world, the camera, the shake, input still waiting, and the game's own state (`Game.snapshot`). Plain JSON. Taken once the las…\n- `restore(saved: Snapshot): Promise<void>`: Put the running level back to a snapshot of it: the same game (by name), already loaded; nothing in between is replayed. The level's recording starts again from it (`Recording.from`), so what is played next replays with `replay()`. Live input held now is let…\n- `fingerprint(): string`: A hash of the simulation: game time, every physics body (exact), `ctx.random`'s state and the game's own (`Game.fingerprint`). Two runs that should be the same (a replay) compare equal. Not the `status()` line: a debug line may carry measurements (a step's mi…\n\n### interface Game\n\nA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\nlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly args?: unknown`: The plain JSON the game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by): kept in its recordings (`Recording.args`), so a tool can make the same game again to replay one (`npm run replay`, src/games.…\n- `readonly assets?: readonly string[]`: Models `setup` will load (`ctx.loadModel` URLs). Optional: listing them lets the engine start downloading while the renderer and physics initialise.\n- `setup(ctx: GameContext): Promise<void> | void`: Build the level: load models, add colliders, spawn entities.\n- `fixedUpdate?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs before every fixed 60 Hz physics step. Move characters/apply forces here.\n- `ready?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is…\n- `update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state).\n- `cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: World position the camera follows.\n- `eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up).\n- `status?(ctx: GameContext): string`: One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...).\n- `fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string`: The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay mu…\n- `snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown`: The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\n- `restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>`: Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\n- `beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>`: Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `re…\n- `onCameraChange?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world, physics and player are untouched; adjust anything that depends on the preset (e.g. side-scroller lane lock, hiding the player model in first person).\n- `dispose?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called when the game is unloaded (`engine.loadGame(other)` or `engine.dispose()`), before the engine removes its scene objects, physics bodies, triggers, particles, HUD and music. Free anything else the game owns: DOM, timers, listeners, HMR hooks.\n\n### interface GameContext\n\n- `readonly engine: Engine`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n\n### class Input\n\nKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n  a frame is not lost.\n- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n\n- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play read…\n- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keep…\n- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n\n### interface InputFrame\n\nWhat the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\nOptional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\nfrom the frame before.\n\n- `dt: number`: Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it).\n- `held?: string[]`: Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted.\n- `pressed?: string[]`: Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held).\n- `analog?: [number, number]`: The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone).\n- `pad?: [number, number]`\n- `delta?: [number, number]`: Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame.\n- `wheel?: number`\n- `pointer?: [number, number, number, number]`: Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says).\n### class AudioManager\n\nWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\nsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\npersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n\nBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\nfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\nwas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n\n- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n- `context: AudioContext | null`\n- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n- `get muted: boolean`\n- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n- `stopMusic(): void`\n- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n\n### interface AudioSettings\n\n- `master: number`\n- `sfx: number`\n- `music: number`\n- `muted: boolean`\n\n### type Instrument\n\n```ts\ntype Instrument = Omit<SoundDef, 'freq' | 'freqEnd' | 'sustain' | 'delay'>\n```\n\nA track's instrument: a SoundDef without pitch or length (each note sets those).\n\n### interface Listener\n\n- `position: V3Like`\n- `right: V3Like`: The listener's right, in world space (unit): the camera's right for a third-person game.\n\n### muffleCutoff\n\n```ts\nconst muffleCutoff: (muffle: number) => number\n```\n\nLow-pass cutoff (Hz) for a muffle of 0..1: wide open to a dull thud.\n\n### noteFrequency()\n\n```ts\nfunction noteFrequency(note: string): number | null\n```\n\n'A4' → 440, 'C#5', 'Eb3'… Returns null for anything that is not a note.\n\n### parseSong()\n\n```ts\nfunction parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong\n```\n\nFlatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos.\n\n### PLAYGROUND_SONG\n\n```ts\nconst PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song\n```\n\nThe playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps.\n\n### interface PlayOptions\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo.\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n\n### renderSound()\n\n```ts\nfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n```\n\nRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n\n### SFX\n\n```ts\nconst SFX: { jump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; attack: number; sustain: number; decay: number; volume: number; }; doubleJump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; ... 5 more ...; volume: number; }; ... 8 more ...; fanfare: { ...; }; }\n```\n\nBuilt-in retro sound effects, as data. Tweak numbers here (or register your own with\n`ctx.audio.register(name, def)`) and every `ctx.audio.play(name)` picks them up.\nSee docs/engine/audio.md for the SoundDef fields.\n\n### type SfxName\n\n```ts\ntype SfxName = keyof typeof SFX\n```\n\n### interface Song\n\nA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\nwhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\nEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\nnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n\n- `bpm: number`\n- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n\n### interface SoundDef\n\n- `wave: Wave`\n- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n- `sustain?: number`\n- `decay?: number`\n- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n- `arpRate?: number`\n- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n\n### interface Spatial\n\n- `gain: number`: 0..1 volume.\n- `pan: number`: -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n- `distance: number`\n\n### spatialize()\n### class Hud\n\nPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\nthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\nlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n\nRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\nresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n\n  ctx.hud.clear();\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n\n- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n- `visible: boolean`\n- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n- `get height: number`\n- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### type HudAnchor\n\n```ts\ntype HudAnchor = 'top-left' | 'top' | 'top-right' | 'left' | 'center' | 'right' | 'bottom-left' | 'bottom' | 'bottom-right'\n```\n\nWhere an element is attached on screen; it is also aligned that way (right-anchored text grows left).\n\n### type HudColor\n\n```ts\ntype HudColor = PaletteColor | number\n```\n\n### interface HudOptions\n\n- `color?: HudColor`: Default 'white'.\n- `shadow?: HudColor | false`: 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'.\n- `scale?: number`: Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1.\n- `anchor?: HudAnchor`: Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels.\n\n### hudPlace()\n\n```ts\nfunction hudPlace(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, anchor: HudAnchor, size: Resolution): [number, number]\n```\n\nTop-left corner, in whole art pixels, of a w×h element placed at offset (x, y) from\n`anchor` on a screen of `size` art pixels. Pure (unit-tested).\n\n## 11. Models and textures (ASSETS.md)\n\n### addModelColliders()\n\n```ts\nfunction addModelColliders(physics: Physics, build: Pick<ModelBuild, 'colliders'>, at: Vec3 = [0, 0, 0], yaw = 0): RAPIER.Collider[]\n```\n\nStatic Rapier colliders for a placed model (its `colliders` data), at `at` turned `yaw`\nradians about Y: a box, an upright cylinder or a convex hull, with `addStaticBox`'s friction.\n\n### around()\n\n```ts\nfunction around(n: number, part: Each<[i: number]>, o: { r?: number; phase?: number } = {}): Part[]\n```\n\n`n` copies turned about Y, `360 / n` degrees apart from `phase`. `r` first pushes each copy\nout along its own +Z; with the default 0 the part's own `at` places it.\n\n### assetProgress()\n### interface ClipDef\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n- `loop?: boolean`\n- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instead of sinking.\n- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n- `notes?: string`\n\n### interface ClipReport\n\nObjective checks for a clip, computed by actually posing the rig (no renderer). These\nare the numbers an agent can't eyeball: foot sliding, ground contact, loop seams,\nvelocity spikes, joint limits. Thresholds are deliberately forgiving; `problems` are\nreal defects, `warnings` are worth a look.\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`\n- `loop: boolean`\n- `speed: number | null`\n- `minSoleY: number`: Lowest sole point over the clip (m) and the frame it happens. Negative = through the floor.\n- `minSoleFrame: number`\n- `minBodyY: number`: Lowest point of any other body part (knees, hands, head...), where and when.\n- `minBodyMesh: string`\n- `minBodyFrame: number`\n- `floatingFrames: number`: Frames (of grounded clips) where neither sole is within 5 cm of the floor.\n- `footSlide: number`: Mean horizontal slip of the planted foot, m/s (0 = perfectly planted).\n- `loopSeam: number`: Largest joint-angle jump between the last and first frame of a loop, degrees.\n- `maxAngularSpeed: { joint: string; degPerSec: number; frame: number }`: Fastest joint rotation, degrees per second, and where.\n- `limitViolations: { joint: string; axis: 'x' | 'y' | 'z'; frame: number; value: number; range: [number, number] }[]`\n- `pelvisY: [number, number]`: Pelvis height range (m) — how much the body bobs.\n- `problems: string[]`\n- `warnings: string[]`\n\n### compileClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction compileClip(def: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec, rest: RestPose): AnimationClip\n```\n\nBake a ClipDef into a three.js AnimationClip (quaternion + position + scale tracks,\nsampled at 60 Hz so easing and per-axis Euler interpolation — including 360° flips —\nsurvive exactly).\n\n### compileClips()\n\n```ts\nfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n```\n\n### interface Foot\n\n- `readonly at: Vec3`: World position of the foot now.\n- `t: number`: Stepping: 0 → 1 through the step; -1 planted.\n- `readonly from: Vec3`\n- `readonly to: Vec3`\n\n### interface FootGoal\n\nFoot-placement IK: say where a foot should be, get the hip/knee/ankle rotations.\n\nAuthors write the body (pelvis height, lean, arms...) and then pin the feet:\n\n  placeFeet({ Pelvis: { p: [0, -0.2, 0] }, Torso: [20, 0, 0] }, rig, {\n    R: { z: 0.15 },                             // right foot flat, 15 cm ahead\n    L: { z: -0.2, pitch: 30, pivot: 'ball' },   // left heel raised on its toes\n  })\n\nCoordinates are the character's ground frame: y up from the floor, z forward. The solve\nis planar (legs bend about X, knees forward); lateral offsets are ignored. It accounts\nfor the root's position, rotation and squash, so a squashed or leaning body still\nkeeps its feet exactly where they were asked…\n\n- `z: number`: Forward position of the ankle (m) when the foot is flat.\n- `x?: number`: Sideways position (m, + = the character's left). Default: under the hip at rest. Only matters when the root is turned (e.g. a pivot on one foot); the solve itself stays in the leg's plane.\n- `y?: number`: Height of the sole above the floor (m). 0 = standing on it.\n- `pitch?: number`: Sole pitch in degrees: + = toes down / heel raised, − = toes up.\n- `pivot?: 'heel' | 'ball' | 'ankle'`: Point that stays at (z, y) while pitching: heel (heel strike), toe tip (tiptoe, toe-off) or ankle.\n- `follow?: number`: 0..1: let the foot hang from the shin instead of holding its world pitch (swinging feet, dangling in the air). 1 = foot at `relax` degrees relative to the shin.\n- `relax?: number`: Foot angle relative to the shin when following (default 15 = toes slightly down).\n\n### gaitClip()\n### validateClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction validateClip(clip: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec): string[]\n```\n\nProblems with a clip definition itself (unknown joints, bad key order, loop mismatch).\n\n## 13. Hero kit\n\n### HERO_CLIPS\n\n```ts\n### applySky()\n\n```ts\nfunction applySky(engine: Pick<Engine, 'sun' | 'ambient' | 'scene' | 'setSunDirection'>, s: SkyState): void\n```\n\nPut a sky on the engine: sun direction, colour and strength, ambient light, background.\n\n### bayer4()\n\n### class Decals\n\n- `new Decals(scene: Scene, o: { capacity?: number } = {})`\n- `readonly meshes: Map<\"ring\" | \"crack\" | \"footprint\" | \"scorch\" | \"splat\", InstancedMesh<BufferGeometry<NormalBufferAttributes, BufferGeometryEventMap>, Material<...> | Material…`\n- `readonly o: { capacity?: number }`\n- `add(shape: DecalShape, at: Vector3Like, normal: Vector3Like, o: DecalOptions = {}): number`: Lay a mark at `at` on a surface facing `normal`. Returns its index in that shape's pool.\n- `count(shape?: DecalShape): number`: Marks alive (per shape or all).\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Per frame: age the marks; old ones dither away over a second.\n- `clear(): void`: Remove every mark (the meshes stay for reuse).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### type DecalShape\n\n```ts\ntype DecalShape = (typeof DECAL_SHAPES)[number]\n```\n\n### decodeState()\n\n```ts\nfunction decodeState(v: unknown): unknown\n```\n\n`encodeState` undone: a deep copy with the marked numbers (and the game's own `@` strings) back.\n\n### drawSheet()\n### class Precipitation\n\n- `new Precipitation(o: PrecipitationOptions)`\n- `readonly kind: PrecipitationKind`\n- `readonly area: readonly [number, number, number]`\n- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind (m/s along x and z): rain slants, snow drifts.\n- `get intensity: number`: Share of the drops falling (0..1).\n- `get speed: number`: Fall speed (m/s).\n- `update(dt: number, center: Vector3): void`: Per frame (game time): advance the fall and follow `center`.\n- `splashes(dt: number): number`: How many drops hit the ground in `dt` seconds at this intensity (for splash particles, ripples).\n- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n\n### type PrecipitationKind\n\n```ts\ntype PrecipitationKind = 'rain' | 'snow'\n```\n\n### interface PrecipitationOptions\n\n- `kind: PrecipitationKind`\n- `count: number`: Most drops at once (the instance count).\n- `area?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Width, height and depth of the air the drops fall through, around the centre.\n- `color?: number`: Drop colour (hex).\n- `speed?: number`: Fall speed (m/s). Rain 16, snow 1.4.\n\n### interface Recording\n\n- `readonly format: 1`\n- `readonly engine: string`: The engine version that recorded it (a different one may not replay the same).\n- `readonly game: string`: The game's name when it was recorded.\n- `readonly args?: unknown`: What the game was made with (`Game.args`), when its name alone can't rebuild it.\n- `readonly seed: number`: The engine seed (`ctx.random` comes from it and the game's name).\n- `readonly time: number`: Game time when the level began (`ctx.time` carries on across levels).\n- `readonly frames: readonly InputFrame[]`: Every frame the level ran, from its first.\n- `readonly truncated?: boolean`: True when the level ran longer than a recording keeps (the frames are its start).\n- `readonly from?: Snapshot`: The save-state the frames start from (`engine.restore()` began this recording), if any.\n- `readonly view?: Viewport`: The viewport it was recorded on, as it was when the level began (CSS pixels and DPR): the art resolution, pointer aim and the HUD's layout follow it. A headless replay frames for it; a page warns when its own differs. A page resized during the level is not fo…\n\n### recordingProblem()\n\n```ts\nfunction recordingProblem(v: unknown): string | null\n```\n\nIs `v` a recording this engine can replay? Returns the problem, or null.\n\n### skyAt()\n\n```ts\nfunction skyAt(hour: number): SkyState\n```\n\nThe sky at `hour` (wraps around 24).\n\n### interface SkyState\n\n- `readonly sunDir: [number, number, number]`: Unit vector toward the sun (or the moon, at night).\n- `readonly sunColor: number`\n- `readonly sunIntensity: number`\n- `readonly ambientColor: number`\n- `readonly ambientIntensity: number`\n- `readonly sky: number`: Background (sky) and fog colour.\n- `readonly night: number`: 0 by day, 1 deep at night: lanterns, stars, windows.\n\n### interface Snapshot\n\n- `readonly format: 1`\n- `readonly engine: string`: The engine version that took it (a different one may not restore the same).\n- `readonly game: string`: The game's name: a snapshot restores into the same game.\n- `readonly seed: number`: The engine seed and game time when it was taken.\n- `readonly time: number`\n- `readonly clock: ClockState`\n- `readonly random: number`: `ctx.random`'s state.\n- `readonly physics: PhysicsState`\n- `readonly camera: CameraRigState`\n- `readonly shake: ShakeState`\n- `readonly input: InputState`\n- `readonly state: unknown`: The game's own state (`Game.snapshot`), as JSON.\n\n### snapshotProblem()\n\n```ts\nfunction snapshotProblem(v: unknown): string | null\n```\n\nIs `v` a snapshot this engine can restore? Returns the problem, or null.\n\n### class SoftMesh\n\n- `new SoftMesh(body: VerletBody, material: Material, o: { uv?: Float32Array; doubleSided?: boolean } = {})`\n- `readonly body: VerletBody`\n- `sync(): void`: Copy the particles into the mesh (call once per frame after stepping the body).\n\n### class SpriteBatch\n### swayObject()\n\n```ts\nfunction swayObject(root: Object3D, wind: WindUniforms, o: SwayOptions): void\n```\n\nMake every mesh under `root` sway (its materials replaced by swaying copies).\n\n### interface SwayOptions\n\n- `height: number`: Height (in the mesh's own units) of its top: the lean is 0 at `base` and full there.\n- `base?: number`: Where the lean starts (the trunk below stays still). Default 0.\n- `amount?: number`: Lean at the top per unit of wind. Default 0.15.\n- `phase?: number`: Its own phase (radians): neighbours don't move in lockstep.\n\n### type Team\n### class WaterSurface\n\n- `new WaterSurface(o: WaterOptions)`\n- `readonly size: readonly [number, number]`\n- `waves: Wave[]`\n- `readonly ripples: RippleField | null`\n- `readonly uOpacity: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n- `readonly uFoamAt: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n- `setWaves(waves: readonly Wave[]): void`: Change the waves (up to MAX_WAVES; the CPU height follows too).\n- `update(time: number, sun?: Vector3): void`: Per frame: the waves' clock (use physics time: floaters read the same), the ripples, the sun.\n- `heightAt(x: number, z: number, t = this.time): number`: World height of the drawn surface at (x, z) (waves and ripples), at the last `update` time or `t`.\n- `covers(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is (x, z) over this water?\n- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n\n### class Wfc\n\n- `new Wfc(tiles: readonly WfcTile[], width: number, height: number, o: WfcOptions = {})`\n- `readonly tiles: readonly WfcTile[]`\n- `readonly wave: Uint8Array`: Options still open per cell: `wave[cell * tiles + t]` is 1 while tile t is possible.\n- `readonly options: Uint16Array`: How many options each cell has left.\n- `readonly result: Int32Array`: The chosen tile per cell (−1 until it has one option left).\n- `attempt: number`: Starts so far (1 + the contradictions met).\n- `done: boolean`: True once every cell has one tile, false while solving or after giving up.\n- `failed: boolean`\n- `choices: number`: Cells collapsed by choice (the rest followed from propagation).\n- `readonly width: number`\n- `readonly height: number`\n- `restart(): void`: Start again from scratch (each start after the first uses the next seed).\n- `run(): boolean`: Solve to the end (restarting on contradictions). True if every cell has a tile.\n- `step(): boolean`: One collapse: the cell with the least entropy gets one tile, and that is propagated. Returns false when there is nothing left to do (solved, or given up).\n- `entropy(c: number): number`: Shannon entropy of a cell's options, by weight (0 when it has one left).\n\n### interface WfcOptions\n\n- `seed?: number`\n- `border?: string`: The socket every edge on the grid's outside must be (a closed map: '###'). Default: any.\n- `fixed?: Readonly<Record<number, number>>`: Cells set before solving: cell index (x + y × width) → tile index (they may break the border: a way in).\n- `attempts?: number`: Starts allowed before giving up on contradictions (default 30).\n\n### interface WfcTile\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly edges: readonly [string, string, string, string]`: North (−z, up the map), east (+x), south (+z), west (−x).\n- `readonly weight?: number`: How often it is chosen, relative to the others (default 1).\n\n### class WindUniforms\n\n- `readonly time: any`\n- `readonly wind: any`\n- `update(time: number, wind: Vector2 | readonly [number, number]): void`: Per frame (game time); `wind` along x and z (its length is the strength).\n\n### woodMaterial()\n\n```ts\nfunction woodMaterial(o: ShaderOptions = {}): MeshToonNodeMaterial\n### class Rng\n\nSeeded randomness. Everything random in a game comes from an `Rng`, so the same seed always\ngives the same result (docs/DOCTRINE.md, principle 1). Each level gets one, `ctx.random`,\nseeded from the engine's seed and the game's name; fork it by purpose so streams stay\nindependent of how many numbers each one drew:\n\n  const level = ctx.random.fork(`level:${n}`);\n  const room = level.fork(`room:${i}`);   // independent of how many numbers `level` drew\n\nNever use Math.random in game or engine code (the lint refuses it).\n\n- `new Rng(seed: number | string)`\n- `get state: number`: The generator's whole state: save it, and set it back to draw the same numbers again.\n- `next(): number`: Uniform in [0, 1). mulberry32.\n- `fork(purpose: string | number): Rng`: A child generator for one purpose; does not advance this one.\n- `range(min: number, max: number): number`: Float in [min, max).\n- `int(min: number, max: number): number`: Integer in [min, max] (inclusive).\n- `chance(p: number): boolean`\n- `pick(items: readonly T[]): T`\n- `weighted(items: readonly T[], weight: (item: T) => number): T`: Pick by weight; entries with weight <= 0 never come up.\n- `shuffle(items: T[]): T[]`\n- `gaussian(mean = 0, sd = 1): number`: Normal distribution (Box–Muller).\n\n### class RopeMesh\n\n- `new RopeMesh(body: VerletBody, material: Material, thickness = 0.06)`\n- `readonly body: VerletBody`\n- `sync(): void`\n\n### SCREEN_LAYER\n\n```ts\nconst SCREEN_LAYER: 2\n```\n\nThe layer for screens and mirrors: drawn by the main camera, never by a RenderView.\n\n### class ScreenFx\n### class ScreenFx\n\n- `cover(kind: TransitionKind = 'fade', o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover the picture (progress → 1). Resolves when it stops: `true` when it is fully covered, `false` when another transition (or `set`, `clear`) took over first.\n- `reveal(kind?: TransitionKind, o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Uncover it (progress → 0) with `kind` (default: the one that covered it). Resolves like `cover`.\n- `transition(kind: TransitionKind, between: () => unknown, o: TransitionOptions & { revealCenter?: TransitionOptions['center'] } = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover, run `between` (load a level, teleport), then reveal, also when `between` throws (the error is passed on). Resolves to whether the cover completed.\n- `set(kind: TransitionKind | null, progress: number): void`: Jump straight to a progress (0 clear, 1 covered), no animation.\n- `flash(color: ScreenColor = 'white', o: FlashOptions = {}): void`: A full-screen flash that fades out.\n- `shockwave(at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number], o: ShockwaveOptions = {}): void`: A ring that pushes the picture outwards from `at` (oldest ring is replaced when 4 run).\n- `clear(): void`: Stop everything (a clear picture).\n- `state(): ScreenFxState`\n- `update(dt: number, camera: Camera | null): void`: Engine, every rendered frame (real seconds; `camera` projects world centres).\n- `sampleNode(tex: any, scale: any): any`: Sample an art-resolution picture for the device pass, pushed by the shockwaves and blocked up by a mosaic transition, with the rings' bright edge. `tex` is the art target (a texture node); `scale` is device pixels per art pixel.\n- `colorNode(color: any, scale: any): any`: The transition and the flash over a finished colour (device pass).\n\n### interface ScreenFxState\n\nWhat `state()` reports (tests, tools).\n\n- `transition: TransitionKind | null`\n- `progress: number`: 0 clear … 1 covered.\n- `moving: boolean`: A transition is animating.\n- `flash: number`\n- `shockwaves: number`\n\n### interface ShaderOptions\n\n- `color?: number`\n- `time?: any`: The clock it animates by (a float node). Default three's `time`, in seconds.\n\n### interface ShakeState\n\nA shake as data.\n\n- `readonly trauma: number`\n- `readonly t: number`\n- `readonly offset: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly applied: readonly number[]`\n\n### interface ShockwaveOptions\n\n- `radius?: number`: Final radius in screen heights. Default 0.45.\n- `duration?: number`: Seconds (real time). Default 0.6.\n- `strength?: number`: Push strength (1 = a strong blast). Default 1.\n\n### skyAt()\n","numLines":805,"startLine":1,"totalLines":805}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SzRwMvZ6irGmub2npPSUCV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
